  "worked_solution": "First, we take ${log}_{4}$ in both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x+1} & ={7}^{x} \\\\ {log}_{4}\u2061({4}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{4}\u2061({7}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{4}\u2061({4}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{4}\u2061({7}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{4}\u2061({7}^{x}) \\\\ x+1 & =x{log}_{4}\u2061(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{4}\u2061(7) \\\\ x-x{log}_{4}\u2061(7) & =-1 \\\\ x(1-{log}_{4}\u2061(7)) & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{1-{log}_{4}\u2061(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
